      "text": "Now, let's be honest about something uncomfortable. We did this. Not the number above the head, something close enough that the difference matters. Look at what a social media feed actually is. It's a bank balance you choose to display. Not your savings, your spending. The holiday, the dinner, the car, the renovation, the concert. It's a curated financial signal broadcast continuously to everyone you've ever met. We built the performance voluntarily, and we built it with the same side effect. Because once everyone is publishing a version of their spending, everyone else is comparing to it. The number isn't in your bank account, it's in the lifestyle, and the lifestyle is public. So the honest version of this thought experiment isn't, what if money became public? It's, what if the version of money we already made public, the performance of money, became accurate instead? Because right now, the public number is a lie told in images. The bloke with the leased car and the borrowed holiday looks like he's winning. The person with the real savings and no posts looks like they're not in the game at all. That's the actual distortion. Not that we hide our money, that we're already showing it badly.",
